WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:03.360
(fast-paced chimes)
- I love bread.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.180
- There is so much good bread in France.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.130
- [Narrator] And although man
does not live by bread alone,

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.663
without it, a meal seems incomplete.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.040
- On every corner, there is
a bakery that is pumping out

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.800
delicious, fresh, well made bread.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:20.633
- [Announcer] It's so fresh.

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:22.680
- This is not easily available to me,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.240
and I want to know why.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.430
Why is it that the bread
that I can get easily

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.160
looks very, very different?

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.040
Why is it that the US
sucks at making bread?

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.661
In fact, let me just show
you what that looks like.

00:00:34.661 --> 00:00:37.320
(video rewinding)

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.640
Okay, wait, it's 12 hours
earlier, still back in the US.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.525
I'm actually at a grocery store right now.

00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:46.108
(gentle music)

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.874
- This is how a lot of us
Americans get our bread.

00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:54.450
(indistinct voices)
Sigh.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.490
My favorite part is when
they make this plastic

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.000
look like it's steaming,
it's just like foggy plastic,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.730
to be like, "this was
just baked right now."

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.027
And it's like, "no,
this was actually baked

00:01:04.027 --> 00:01:08.190
"like three weeks ago in a
factory in like Connecticut."

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.560
- [Speaker] It's even made
with real butter. (laughing)

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.042
- [Announcer] Always buy Wonder Bread.

00:01:12.042 --> 00:01:14.280
(cash register chiming)
You'll be glad you did.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.840
- The reason I'm purchasing this bread

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.050
is because I want to bring
it with me to France,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.850
just to like have an example lesson,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.110
and I may use it as a pillow,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.224
'cause it's literally as
soft as a Novaform pillow.

00:01:25.224 --> 00:01:27.600
(snoring)

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.940
Some of this bagged bread
is made with ingredients

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.209
that are literally illegal in the EU.

00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:35.010
(machine beeps)
Back to France. Let's do it.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.036
- There is nothing more
American than Wonder Bread.

00:01:37.036 --> 00:01:41.901
(upbeat music)
(machines beeping)

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.523
- [Narrator] (speaking
foreign language) France.

00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:50.161
(light accordion music)

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.290
- In case you're wondering, yes.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.320
Any video from Paris must
include music like this.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.050
Nice, gentle, cafe accordion music.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.960
There's my composer,
Tom, making it right now.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.000
- It's pretty cool.
- It's just so good.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.910
Okay, so yeah, we know that
France is good at bread

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.710
and the US sucks at it.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.230
Is this just another video
where I shit on the USA

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.060
for being terrible at certain things?

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.860
Yes, it is.
(music winding down)

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.540
That's exactly what it
is, but hear me out.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.650
I actually have something to say here.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.070
I believe that bread is
a really important symbol

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.190
for a bigger cultural
phenomenon in the US,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.780
and that's what I want
to talk about today,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.500
where industrialized bread
came from, why it exists,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.783
and how some people are
trying to change it.

00:02:33.783 --> 00:02:34.616
(music continues)

00:02:34.616 --> 00:02:36.390
I'll get to that explanation, but first,

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.270
I'm gonna go into that bakery
over there and buy myself

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:43.530
a large ball of butter and
flour stuffed with chocolate.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.090
Oh, and Tom, can you throw in a beat

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.621
to this accordion music, please?

00:02:46.621 --> 00:02:47.454
- Yeah!

00:02:47.454 --> 00:02:48.287
- Thanks.
(upbeat accordion music)

00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:49.569
- [Speaker] Any questions?

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:53.095
- Yes. Are you going to
finish that "quassoint"?

00:02:53.095 --> 00:02:55.928
(music continues)

00:02:57.281 --> 00:02:58.294
Oh, yeah.

00:02:58.294 --> 00:03:00.600
(music fades out)

